<article class="post-body">

<!-- §01 — The Erasing Dilemma -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Erasing Dilemma</div>
<h2>A Small Edit, a Huge Recomputation Bill</h2>
<p>Modern LLMs in agentic pipelines don't process static prompts — they accumulate context online: retrieved passages, tool results, conversation turns, imported skill files. Problems are discovered late. A RAG system might prefill 30,000 tokens before realizing that one 100-token retrieved passage contains a stale fact. A tool-augmented assistant might cache an observation before detecting a wrong result. A user retracts a preference after the dialogue context is already processed.</p>
<p>In all these cases, a <strong>short problematic span</strong> is identified only after prefill, when the long context has already been written into the KV cache. The desired operation is <em>context erasing</em>: make future decoding behave as if that span had never appeared.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">context-erasing-cost.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag the slider to move the erased span</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="pos-slider-row">
      <label>Span position in context:</label>
      <input type="range" id="pos-slider" min="5" max="85" value="25" step="5">
      <label id="pos-label" style="min-width:80px">early (25%)</label>
    </div>
    <canvas id="cost-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div style="margin-top:.75rem">
      <div class="cost-row">
        <span class="cost-label">Exact recompute</span>
        <div class="cost-track">
          <div class="cost-fill" id="cost-exact" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,.7)">
            <span id="cost-exact-lbl">suffix: 75% of context</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cost-row">
        <span class="cost-label">KVEraser</span>
        <div class="cost-track">
          <div class="cost-fill" id="cost-kve" style="background:rgba(139,92,246,.7);width:10%">
            <span>span: 10%</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cache-legend" style="margin-top:.75rem">
      <span class="cl-prefix">Prefix (valid)</span>
      <span class="cl-span">Erased span e</span>
      <span class="cl-suffix">Suffix (contaminated)</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The exact solution — rerunning prefill over the entire suffix <em>s</em> after the deleted span — has computational cost O(|s|(|p|+|s|)). This is governed by the <strong>suffix length</strong>, not the deleted-span length. When a 100-token passage is erased early in a 32K-token context, the suffix can be 31,000 tokens — making exact erasure essentially as expensive as processing the entire document from scratch.</p>
<p>Simpler alternatives fail: instruction-only forgetting ("please ignore X") leaves the cache unchanged, so later queries compete with cached evidence rather than rewriting it. Simply deleting the span's KV entries corrupts downstream states (see §02). KVEraser solves this by <strong>learning to generate replacement KV states</strong> for the erased interval — paying only for the span, not the suffix.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 — KV Contamination -->
<section id="s-contamination" data-code="contamination">
<div class="section-no">02 — Why You Can't Just Delete the Span's KV</div>
<h2>The Contamination Problem</h2>
<p>To understand why deleting the span's KV entries fails, recall how causal attention works. When token <em>t</em> is processed, its key and value vectors are computed attending to all previous tokens. Those K/V vectors are then cached. Critically, this means the K/V state of token <em>t</em> permanently encodes the influence of everything before it — including any later-erased span <em>e</em>.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">contamination-propagation.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click buttons to see what each approach does</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:1rem">
      <button class="step-nav" id="btn-original" onclick="showContamState('original')" style="padding:.3rem .7rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--purple);background:var(--purple-dim);color:var(--purple-l)">Original context</button>
      <button class="step-nav" id="btn-delete" onclick="showContamState('delete')" style="padding:.3rem .7rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2)">Delete-and-shift</button>
      <button class="step-nav" id="btn-kve" onclick="showContamState('kve')" style="padding:.3rem .7rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2)">KVEraser</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="contam-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block"></canvas>
    <p id="contam-desc" style="font-size:.75rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.75rem;font-style:italic;line-height:1.55">Suffix tokens "attended to" the erased span during prefill. Their cached K/V states carry that influence forever — even if the span's own entries are removed.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The concrete example: suppose the context contains "The capital of France is London. [followed by 1,000 more tokens…] Question: What is the capital of France?" Even if we delete "London"'s KV entries, all 1,000 suffix tokens were encoded while attending to "London". Their cached states still "believe" "London" was there. The model will answer incorrectly.</p>
<p>More formally, the KV cache decomposes as <strong>KV(x) = KV<sub>prefix</sub> ⊕ KV<sub>e</sub> ⊕ KV<sub>suffix</sub></strong>. After deleting span e, KV<sub>prefix</sub> remains valid — but KV<sub>suffix</sub> is contaminated: every suffix token attended to e during its own encoding. Exact erasing must recompute KV<sub>suffix</sub> from scratch under the edited prompt. KVEraser instead asks: can we <em>compensate</em> for that contamination by replacing KV<sub>e</sub> with learned steering states?</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 — KVEraser Architecture -->
<section id="s-architecture" data-code="architecture">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Surrogate Cache</div>
<h2>Steering States as a Compensation Interface</h2>
<p>KVEraser's key insight: the erased span's KV positions (<em>m</em> to <em>n</em>) are the only positions where query tokens and newly-decoded tokens directly attend to the erased content. By replacing those positions with <strong>learned steering states</strong>, KVEraser can compensate for the residual contamination carried by the reused suffix — without touching the suffix at all.</p>

