<p>The eraser module E<sub>φ</sub> is implemented as a <strong>trainable copy of the generator backbone</strong> (without the LM head). It is conditioned on two inputs: the preserved prefix KV cache KV<sub>1:m-1</sub>, and the erased span text e. It outputs per-layer, per-head key and value tensors for positions m through n — the steering states that replace the original erased-span KV entries.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Surrogate cache construction</div>
  <span class="math-var">KV̂(x; m, n)</span> = <span class="math-var">KV<sub>1:m-1</sub>(x)</span> ⊕ <span class="math-var">KV_steer(x; m, n)</span> ⊕ <span class="math-var">KV<sub>n+1:T</sub>(x)</span><br>
  <span class="math-comment">       prefix (valid)         steering (learned)          suffix (contaminated, compensated by steering)</span>
</div>

<p>The critical design choice: <strong>the eraser is query-agnostic</strong>. It doesn't see the downstream query <em>u</em> or the suffix <em>s</em>. It only sees the prefix and the span to erase. This makes the steering states reusable across different queries on the same erased context, and avoids requiring the computationally expensive suffix as input — exactly what we're trying to avoid processing.</p>

<h3>Why can this local edit work?</h3>
<p>A query token (or newly decoded token) can attend to the erased span's positions directly, or to suffix positions whose states were computed attending to the span. KVEraser uses positions m through n as a "compensation interface": the steering states are trained so that the surrogate cache produces the same decoding behavior as the clean edited prompt — without reconstructing the exact edited suffix cache.</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 — Training Pipeline -->
<section id="s-training" data-code="training">
<div class="section-no">04 — Teaching the Eraser</div>
<h2>Two-Stage Training for Transferable Erasing</h2>
<p>Large-scale annotated data for context erasing don't exist, so KVEraser uses a two-stage strategy designed to learn a transferable erasing mechanism. Stage 1 pre-trains on easily constructed synthetic data. Stage 2 fine-tunes on a small downstream set.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">training-pipeline.steps</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Prev / Next to walk through the pipeline</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="training-steps">
      <div class="step-box active" id="tstep-0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Construct pre-training data</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Randomly insert a 100-token Wikipedia chunk (span e) into a long Wikipedia document. Select an anchor string in the retained context; the task is to retrieve the text immediately neighboring the anchor — which requires suppressing e when the anchor is adjacent to it.</div>
          <div class="step-code">80K samples · Wikipedia documents · hybrid BM25 + dense retrieval</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="tstep-1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Stage 1: Span-neighbor pre-training</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Train the eraser with the teacher-forced objective: given the surrogate cache (with steering states replacing e), the frozen generator must produce the correct neighbor text. When the anchor is adjacent to e, success requires neutralizing e's influence. When the anchor is elsewhere, the eraser must preserve non-erased content.</div>
          <div class="step-code">Lerase(φ) = -Σ log pθ(aₜ | KV̂φ(x;m,n), u, a&lt;t)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="tstep-2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Stage 2: Task-specific fine-tuning</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Fine-tune on ~7.5K downstream erasing samples: (a) Erasing Needle-in-a-Haystack (NIAH) — two needles share the same key, different values; erase the earlier one. (b) QA with harmful distractors — randomly inserted Wikipedia passages that cause incorrect answers. Only retain samples where the distractor causes failure.</div>
          <div class="step-code">~7.5K samples · NIAH (&#123;1K–32K&#125; × 6 sizes) + NQ, TriviaQA, HotpotQA</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="tstep-3">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Inference: local KV steering</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Given a processed context and a span to erase, the frozen eraser generates steering states for positions m:n in O(|e|(|p|+|e|)) time. The surrogate cache is assembled: prefix KV (reused) + steering states (new) + suffix KV (reused). The frozen generator decodes from this surrogate cache.</div>
          <div class="step-code">cost = O(|e| × (|p| + |e|))  — independent of suffix length |s|</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="tstep-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <span id="tstep-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 4</span>
      <button id="tstep-next">Next →</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Training objective</h3>
<p>The key property of the loss: the <strong>generator is frozen</strong>, only the eraser weights φ are updated. The objective encourages the surrogate cache to induce the same decoding behavior as the clean edited prompt — not to reconstruct the exact edited KV states token-by-token, but to approximate the counterfactual decoding distribution.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Teacher-forced training loss</div>
  ℒ_erase(<span class="math-var">φ</span>) = −<span class="math-var">Σ</span> log p<sub>θ</sub>(<span class="math-var">aₜ</span> | <span class="math-var">KV̂<sup>φ</sup>(x; m, n)</span>, <span class="math-var">u</span>, <span class="math-var">a<sub>&lt;t</sub></span>)<br>
  <span class="math-comment">  θ frozen (generator)    φ updated (eraser)    a = response from clean edited prompt x̃</span>
</div>

<h3>What not to condition the eraser on</h3>
<p>The ablation study (Table 1 in the paper) tests three alternative conditioning variants. Removing the prefix KV hurts average exact match by 2 pp. Adding query conditioning gives inconsistent gains. Adding suffix KV conditioning (motivated by local suffix repair) also doesn't help. The paper's simple query-agnostic design — prefix KV + erased span text — captures the most useful information, and keeps inference cost bounded by span length rather than suffix length.</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 — Scaling -->
<section id="s-scaling" data-code="scaling">
<div class="section-no">05 — Scaling Analysis</div>
<h2>24% vs 17.6× — The Span vs Suffix Divide</h2>
<p>The theoretical advantage of KVEraser is clear: its cache-construction cost is O(|e|(|p|+|e|)), while full recomputation is O(|s|(|p|+|s|)). Since the erased span is typically much shorter than the suffix, KVEraser should scale far better. The experiments confirm this dramatically.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">latency-vs-context-size.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">latency (log scale) vs context size — NIAH benchmark, 100 samples each</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="scaling-canvas" style="width:100%;height:280px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:1.25rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:.75rem;font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">
      <span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.35rem"><span style="width:18px;height:2px;background:#ef4444;display:inline-block"></span><span style="color:var(--muted2)">Full recompute (+17.6×)</span></span>
      <span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.35rem"><span style="width:18px;height:2px;background:#8b5cf6;display:inline-block"></span><span style="color:var(--muted2)">KVEraser (+24%)</span></span>
      <span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.35rem"><span style="width:18px;height:2px;background:#6b7280;display:inline-block;opacity:.7"></span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Delete-and-shift</span></span>
      <span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.35rem"><span style="width:18px;height:2px;background:#f59e0b;display:inline-block"></span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Instruction-only</span></span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>From 1K to 32K context, KVEraser's latency increases by only <strong>24%</strong> — because the erased span remains a fixed 100-token chunk regardless of total context length. Full recomputation grows <strong>17.6×</strong> over the same range, as the suffix grows from ~500 tokens to ~16,000 tokens. The approximate baselines offer no relief: delete-and-shift and instruction-only forgetting also scale poorly, and local suffix repair can even exceed full recompute at small context sizes when erasing fails (the model generates both needle values, doubling decoding time).</p>

<p>The asymmetry captures the core problem precisely: if you identify a bad span <em>early</em> in a long context, full recompute must still process the entire remaining context — 32K tokens in the worst case. KVEraser always pays only for the span size.</p>
</section>

<!-- §06 — Results -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results</div>
<h2>Near-Perfect Erasure on Controlled Benchmark, Best Approximate on Natural QA</h2>

<h3>Erasing Needle-in-a-Haystack (NIAH)</h3>
<p>The NIAH benchmark isolates context erasing mechanics. Two needles share the same key but different values: "One of the special magic numbers for [key] is: [value_A]" (inserted early, to erase) and "… is: [value_B]" (inserted later, to retain). After erasing needle A, the model must retrieve only value_B. Because needles use random strings, parametric knowledge cannot help — the model must actually erase the earlier influence.</p>

